# KNOWN GAP (todo/t0963) — ⭐ THE REFERENCE LAGS THE SELF-HOST.
#
# `flat_map` with an implicit-`it` body zeroes every element after the first of
# each inner vector, on Rust `gg`. The self-host gets it RIGHT.
#
# Three spellings of the SAME argument. Two are correct on both lanes; the
# third is correct only on the self-host:
#
#   lit.flat_map((Vector[int] v): v)   Rust 1 1 2 2   SH 1 1 2 2
#   lit.flat_map(ident)                Rust 1 1 2 2   SH 1 1 2 2
#   lit.flat_map(it)                   Rust 1 0 2 0   SH 1 1 2 2   <-- here
#
# The element COUNT is right and only the values are wrong, so any test
# asserting a length stays green through it.
#
# `flat_map` has its own `comp_reserve` arm (the nested-loop lowering,
# `v.flat_map(f)` == `[y for x in v for y in f(x)]`). Two of the three
# spellings reach it correctly, so the fault is in what the implicit-`it` path
# hands that arm, not in the flattening. The surviving-first-element pattern
# points at the INNER loop's element read.
#
# ⚠ THE ASSERTED OUTPUT IS THE CORRECT ONE, NEVER THE REFERENCE'S CURRENT
# ANSWER. Per the succession plan: file a "reference lags the self-host"
# finding and fix the Rust side as oracle hygiene — never dumb the self-host
# down to match. Un-ignore when Rust `gg` prints `1 1 2 2` for all three.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the #[ignore]d wired test): all three groups print
# 1 1 2 2.

Vector[int] ident(Vector[int] v):
    return v

void main():
    Vector[Vector[int]] lit = [[1, 1], [2, 2]]
    Vector[int] a = lit.flat_map((Vector[int] v): v)
    for x in a:
        print(f"closure {x}")
    Vector[int] b = lit.flat_map(ident)
    for x in b:
        print(f"named {x}")
    Vector[int] c = lit.flat_map(it)
    for x in c:
        print(f"it {x}")
